      "side": 1,
      "message": "Sorry if this wasn\u0027t clear to you but the point of a `transparent` struct is that you can (effectively) implement object-oriented methods for pointers. So my suggestion was something along the lines of\n```\n#[repr(transparent)]\nstruct FdtPropertyStruct(libfdt_bindgen::fdt_property);\n\nimpl FdtPropertyStruct {\n    fn name_offset(\u0026self) -\u003e u32 { u32::from_be(self.0.nameoff) }\n    // other fields\n}\n\n// not sure this is necessary\nimpl AsRef\u003cFdtPropertyStruct\u003e for libfdt_bindgen::fdt_property {\n    fn as_ref(\u0026self) -\u003e \u0026FdtPropertyStruct {\n        unsafe { self as _ }\n    }\n}\n```\nso that you can transform the `*const libfdt_bindgen::fdt_property` into a `\u0026FdtPropertyStruct` (free!)\n```\nimpl AsRef\u003cFdtPropertyStruct\u003e for FdtProperty {\n    fn as_ref(\u0026self) -\u003e \u0026FdtPropertyStruct {\n        let ptr \u003d fdt_get_property_by_offset(self.fdt, self.offset).unwrap();\n        // ...\n        unsafe {\u0026*ptr}\n    }\n}\n```\nand start using your methods, transparently taking care of the endianness (e.g. \"`self.as_ref().name_offset()`\") with no overhead!\n\n\u003e  I only wrapped `\u0026libfdt_bindgen::fdt_property` because `libfdt_bindgen::fdt_property` didn\u0027t impl `Copy` while `Copy` is very useful.\n\nPerhaps, are you confusing the structure (by value) and a reference to it (which is always `Copy`)?",
      "parentUuid": "b407118e_cc016b33",
